{
  "id": "proj-knife",
  "slug": "forged-kitchen-knife",
  "status": "published",
  "title": "Forged Kitchen Knife",
  "summary": "A carbon-steel utility knife forged from 1084 bar stock in a two-brick forge, hardened in warm oil, tempered in a kitchen oven and fitted with walnut scales on brass pins.",
  "difficulty": "advanced",
  "estimatedHours": 16,
  "imageUrl": null,
  "currency": "USD",
  "steps": [
    "Stack the two soft firebricks with a channel between them and light the MAP torch into the opening to make the forge.",
    "Heat the bar to orange and forge the point and the bevels with the cross-peen hammer on the anvil block, holding the bar in tongs.",
    "Normalize three times: heat until a magnet no longer sticks, then let the blade cool in still air.",
    "Grind the profile and bevels with the angle grinder, then drill the two handle pin holes before hardening.",
    "Harden: heat to non-magnetic and quench edge-first in canola oil warmed to about 120 F. Check that a file skates on the edge.",
    "Temper in the kitchen oven at 400 F for two one-hour cycles, cooling between them.",
    "Hand-sand the blade from 120 to 400 grit, then glue the walnut scales on with epoxy and peen the brass pins.",
    "Shape and sand the handle, finish it with oil, and hone the edge on the whetstone."
  ],
